---
import PolicyLayout from "../layouts/PolicyLayout.astro";
import { sitePath } from "../lib/site-path";
---

<PolicyLayout title="Code signing policy" description="Chaft's release signing status, maintainer responsibilities, and planned SignPath approval process.">
  <h2>Current signing status</h2>
  <p>
    Windows Authenticode signing through SignPath Foundation is planned. Chaft has
    not yet been accepted into the program and does not currently publish
    SignPath-signed releases. Windows canary packages remain unsigned. Linux
    canary.4 includes a detached OpenPGP signature; macOS canaries are unsigned
    and are not notarized. Check the signing status and evidence for each exact
    package on the <a href={sitePath("/releases/")}>release pages</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Planned SignPath attribution</h2>
  <p>If accepted and once signing is enabled, the following attribution will apply:</p>
  <p>
    Free code signing provided by <a href="https://signpath.io/">SignPath.io</a>,
    certificate by <a href="https://signpath.org/">SignPath Foundation</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Windows certificate would identify SignPath Foundation as its publisher.
    A signature verifies the signing identity and file integrity; it does not
    establish that Chaft has been independently audited or is production ready.
  </p>

  <h2>Maintainer responsibilities</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Committer and reviewer: <a href="https://github.com/Jurshsmith">Jurshsmith</a>, the repository owner. Contributions from other authors require maintainer review before release.</li>
    <li>Signing approver: <a href="https://github.com/Jurshsmith">Jurshsmith</a>. Each SignPath release signing request must receive manual approval.</li>
    <li>Anyone with source or signing access must use multi-factor authentication on GitHub and, when provisioned, SignPath. This is an access requirement, not a claim that SignPath onboarding is complete.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Signing scope and release checks</h2>
  <p>
    The planned workflow will submit traceable GitHub Actions build artifacts
    from the public <a href="https://github.com/Jurshsmith/chaft">Chaft repository</a>.
    Approval must cover the source revision, build workflow, checks, and exact
    artifact. Only Chaft-owned binaries are in scope for its SignPath signing
    policy. Upstream libraries, including Qt DLLs, retain their upstream signing
    state and must not be signed as Chaft-owned code.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before publication, the release workflow must verify the signed files,
    expected certificate identity, timestamps, package contents, and checksums.
    Signing metadata must identify Chaft and the intended product version.
    Signing failures must block publication of releases advertised as signed.
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy and reporting</h2>
  <p>
    Read the <a href={sitePath("/privacy/")}>privacy disclosure</a> for local
    storage, network transfers, and third-party services. Report suspected
    signing misuse or security issues through our
    <a href={sitePath("/security/")}>private security reporting process</a>.
  </p>
</PolicyLayout>
